{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"021ad64c-3057-4a2d-a8f7-b23944ba6f8e","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I've fixed the timezone date bug by completely separating the calendar date (content) from the timestamp used for sorting (metadata), as described in the guide.\n\nHere are the changes made:\n1. **`journal.py`**:\n   - `publish()` now accepts `local_date` (an opaque string provided by the author's client) instead of guessing the date on the server using `client_tz`.\n   - Added a UTC `received_at` timestamp in `publish()` strictly for feed ordering.\n   - Removed the timezone conversion logic in `display_date()` so it always returns the author's local date exactly as it was captured, completely unmodified.\n   - Updated `feed()` to sort posts primarily by the `received_at` timestamp, falling back to the `date` in case of ties.\n\n2. **`tests/test_journal.py`**:\n   - Updated existing tests to pass `local_date` (e.g., `\"2026-03-10\"`) rather than timezones like `\"UTC\"` or `\"Europe/Berlin\"`.\n   - Added `test_verify_reproduce_scenario` exactly as described in the blog post to verify the bug is fixed. The test confirms that a UTC+9 user and a UTC-3 user submitting locally at 23:30 on the same evening each see their own local date (`2026-07-31`), but their posts appear in the correct chronological order in the feed.\n\n3. **`README.md`**:\n   - Updated the documentation to reflect the new `publish(entry, local_date)` signature expected by the backend.\n\nThe test suite is passing successfully (`Ran 5 tests in 0.000s - OK`). The code is ready! Let me know if there's anything else you need.\n","duration_seconds":87.747438,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":65378,"output_tokens":9869,"thinking_tokens":6009,"cache_read_tokens":97315,"total_tokens":75247}}}
